Network failures create a subtle problem: the producer sends a record, the broker writes it, but the acknowledgment is lost in transit. The producer retries — now the broker has two copies. `enable.idempotence=true` solves this by assigning each producer a unique Producer ID (PID) and each record a sequence number per partition. The broker detects and discards duplicates automatically.

```java
Properties props = new Properties();
props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");
props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, true);    // deduplicate retries
props.put(ProducerConfig.RETRIES_CONFIG, Integer.MAX_VALUE);  // retry until success
props.put(ProducerConfig.MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS_PER_CONNECTION, 5); // safe with idempotence

// With idempotence, the broker tracks (PID, partition, sequence)
// Duplicate sends get the same offset back — no duplicates in the log
KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props);

producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("payments", userId, payload), (metadata, ex) -> {
    if (ex != null) log.error("Send failed for partition {}", metadata.partition(), ex);
    else log.debug("Offset {} partition {}", metadata.offset(), metadata.partition());
});
```

Idempotence has negligible overhead — there's no good reason to leave it off. Since Kafka 3.0, it's enabled by default.

The most dangerous rebalance side-effect is **duplicate processing**. Here's the sequence: consumer A processes 50 records from partition 3 but hasn't committed the offset yet. A rebalance triggers. Consumer B is now assigned partition 3 and starts reading from the last committed offset — re-processing those 50 records.

Defense strategies:

```java
// Manual offset commit — commit AFTER processing, not before
props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, false);

while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(1000));
    for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
        processRecord(record);  // process first
    }
    consumer.commitSync();      // then commit — at-least-once guarantee
}
```

`enable.auto.commit=true` (default) commits periodically in the background. If a rebalance happens between a commit and your processing, you either lose records (committed but not processed) or reprocess them (processed but not committed). Manual commit with `commitSync()` after processing gives **at-least-once** semantics — duplicates possible, but no data loss.

For exactly-once, use Kafka transactions: `producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction()` atomically commits consumer offsets and producer output in a single transaction.

The idempotent producer handles single-record deduplication. The transactional API handles **multi-partition atomic writes** — either all records commit or none do.

```java
props.put(ProducerConfig.TRANSACTIONAL_ID_CONFIG, "payment-processor-1");
// transactional.id must be unique per app instance and stable across restarts

KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props);
producer.initTransactions(); // register with the transaction coordinator

try {
    producer.beginTransaction();
    producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("payments", key, paymentEvent));
    producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("audit-log", key, auditEvent));
    producer.commitTransaction(); // atomic: both or neither
} catch (Exception e) {
    producer.abortTransaction();  // roll back — neither record is visible
}
```

The broker holds records in a "pending" state. Consumers with default settings (`isolation.level=read_uncommitted`) see pending records — only consumers with `read_committed` wait for the commit.

**Zombie fencing**: if a producer with the same `transactional.id` starts (old instance didn't shut down cleanly), the coordinator fences the old producer — its pending transactions are aborted. This prevents split-brain duplicate writes.

The complete exactly-once pattern: consume records, process them, produce output, and commit consumer offsets — all in a single atomic transaction.

```java
KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(consumerProps);
// consumer: isolation.level = read_committed, enable.auto.commit = false

KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(producerProps);
// producer: transactional.id = "processor-1"

producer.initTransactions();
consumer.subscribe(List.of("input-topic"));

while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
    producer.beginTransaction();
    for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
        String result = process(record);  // your business logic
        producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("output-topic", record.key(), result));
    }
    // Atomically commit: output records + consumer offsets in one transaction
    producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(
        currentOffsets(records), consumer.groupMetadata());
    producer.commitTransaction();
}
```

If the process crashes after `send()` but before `commitTransaction()`, the transaction is aborted on recovery. The consumer offsets aren't committed either, so the records are re-read and reprocessed — but this time without duplicates because the output from the aborted transaction is never visible to `read_committed` consumers.

Kafka Streams and Apache Flink use this pattern internally — you often get EOS "for free" from the framework without writing transactional code yourself.
